% Problem: How Many Taylor Terms Does a Clearance Factor Actually Need?
% Name:
% Date:

clear; clc;

x = -0.8;
tolerance = 1e-6;

% a) Vectorized Lagrange remainder bound over nvals = 5:9 -> nRequired
nvals = 5:9;


% b) For-loop: accumulate the Maclaurin polynomial up to degree nRequired -> approxVal


% c) actualErr, and confirm it's below tolerance


% d) 2-3 sentence written explanation (as a comment)


% e) 2-3 sentence written explanation (as a comment)

solutionCode:
x = -0.8;
tolerance = 1e-6;

% a) Vectorized Lagrange remainder bound
nvals = 5:9;
bounds = abs(x).^(nvals+1) ./ factorial(nvals+1);
disp(bounds)
idxN = find(bounds < tolerance, 1);
nRequired = nvals(idxN);
fprintf('nRequired = %d\n', nRequired);

% b) Accumulate the Maclaurin polynomial term by term
approxVal = 0;
for k = 0:nRequired
    approxVal = approxVal + x.^k / factorial(k);
end
fprintf('approxVal = %.10f\n', approxVal);

% c) Actual error vs. the true value
actualErr = abs(exp(x) - approxVal);
fprintf('actualErr = %.3e (below tolerance: %d)\n', actualErr, actualErr < tolerance);

% d) Written explanation:
% The Lagrange bound uses the WORST-CASE value of e^xi over the entire interval between
% x and 0 (here, its maximum possible value of 1, at xi = 0), but the derivative e^xi is
% actually smaller than 1 everywhere else in that interval (since x is negative), so the
% true remainder is always somewhat less than what the bound conservatively allows for.

% e) Written explanation:
% Tightening the tolerance to 1e-10 would require a LARGER nRequired, never a smaller one,
% because each additional term only ever shrinks the remainder bound further -- the bound
% is a strictly decreasing sequence in n, so demanding a smaller error simply means you have
% to keep adding terms until that stricter threshold is finally crossed.

% a) Elimination half-life
k = 0.15;
halfLife = elimHalfLife(k);
fprintf('Half-life: %.2f hr\n', halfLife);

% b) Peak concentration and its timing
doses = [2.1 5.4 8.9 7.2 4.8 2.6];
times = [0 1 2 4 6 8];
[Cmax, tCmax] = peakConcentration(doses, times);
fprintf('Cmax = %.1f mg/L at t = %d hr\n', Cmax, tCmax);

% c) Anonymous function for a quick one-off check
oneCompartment = @(dose, Vd, k, t) (dose/Vd) * exp(-k*t);
valAt3 = oneCompartment(400, 35, 0.15, 3);
fprintf('Concentration at t=3: %.4f mg/L\n', valAt3);

% d) Written explanation:
% A local function is convenient when the formula is only ever used within this one script,
% since it keeps everything in a single file with no extra bookkeeping. A full standalone
% function file is the right choice once a formula (like elimHalfLife) needs to be called from
% MULTIPLE different scripts in a real project, since MATLAB can find any function file on its
% path regardless of which script is currently running -- a local function, by contrast, is only
% visible inside the file it's defined in. An anonymous function is best for a short, throwaway,
% single-expression formula needed right where it's used (like oneCompartment here), since it
% avoids writing a whole function block, but it becomes awkward for anything needing multiple
% lines, loops, or reuse across several separate script files.

% e) Written explanation:
% MATLAB locates a top-level function by searching its path for a FILE whose name matches the
% function being called, so the file name and function name must agree; local functions, on the
% other hand, are only ever reached by code already inside the same file, so MATLAB finds them
% by name within that file directly and never needs to search the path for them at all.

% --- Local functions ---
function halfLife = elimHalfLife(k)
    halfLife = log(2)/k;
end

function [Cmax, tCmax] = peakConcentration(doses, times)
    [Cmax, idx] = max(doses);
    tCmax = times(idx);
end

