% a) Three sampling resolutions of the same signal
tCoarse = linspace(0,5,6);
tMed = linspace(0,5,26);
tFine = linspace(0,5,501);
sigCoarse = 1 - exp(-tCoarse);
sigMed = 1 - exp(-tMed);
sigFine = 1 - exp(-tFine);

% b) Plot all three together
figure
plot(tFine, sigFine, 'k-', 'LineWidth', 1)
hold on
plot(tMed, sigMed, 'b--o', 'MarkerSize', 4)
plot(tCoarse, sigCoarse, 'r:s', 'MarkerSize', 8, 'LineWidth', 1.5)
hold off
xlabel('Time (s)')
ylabel('Recovery signal')
title('Sampling Density vs. a Continuous Recovery Curve')
legend('Fine (n=501)', 'Medium (n=26)', 'Coarse (n=6)', 'Location', 'southeast')

% c) Closest coarse sample to t = 2.5 and its error
[~, idxClose] = min(abs(tCoarse - 2.5));
trueVal = 1 - exp(-2.5);
absErrClose = abs(trueVal - sigCoarse(idxClose));
fprintf('Closest coarse sample: t = %.2f, absolute error at t=2.5 is %.4f\n', tCoarse(idxClose), absErrClose);

% d) Written justification:
% The coarse curve uses a dotted line with large square markers, the medium curve uses a dashed
% line with small circle markers, and the fine curve uses a solid black line with no markers at
% all -- three different line styles AND marker choices together, not color alone. This matters
% most right near the fine curve, where the medium and coarse samples visually converge onto it;
% relying on color alone there would make it easy to lose track of which points belong to which
% sampling density, while the distinct line style and marker shape stay identifiable regardless.

% e) Written explanation:
% Even the 501-point 'fine' curve is still just a finite set of connected straight-line segments
% approximating the true continuous function 1 - exp(-t), not the function itself -- it is simply
% dense enough that the approximation error becomes visually imperceptible. This mirrors lecture's
% point about exp(x): a computer can only ever evaluate a function at a finite number of points
% and connect them, so more samples always mean a BETTER approximation, never a perfectly exact one.

x = 0.2;
ns = [2 4 6 8 10];
allErrors = zeros(size(ns));
for i = 1:length(ns)
    n = ns(i);
    approx = 0;
    for k = 1:n
        approx = approx + (-1)^(k+1) * x^k / k;
    end
    allErrors(i) = abs(log(1+x) - approx);
end

% b) Display errors and find the first n meeting the tolerance
for i = 1:length(ns)
    fprintf('n = %2d: error = %.3e\n', ns(i), allErrors(i));
end
idxMin = find(allErrors < 1e-8, 1);
nMin = ns(idxMin);
fprintf('nMin = %d\n', nMin);

% c) Written explanation:
% Each additional pair of terms in the Maclaurin series for ln(1+x) adds a smaller and smaller
% correction (since x = 0.2 is less than 1, higher powers of x shrink quickly), so truncating
% the series later leaves a smaller leftover (untruncated) remainder -- exactly the Taylor
% remainder idea from lecture, where the omitted terms bound how far the approximation can be
% from the true value, and that bound shrinks as more correct terms are included.

% d) Round-off error demonstration
bigApprox = log(1 + x + 1e15) - log(1e15);
absErrBig = abs(log(1+x) - bigApprox);
fprintf('bigApprox = %.10f, true = %.10f, absErrBig = %.6e\n', bigApprox, log(1+x), absErrBig);

% e) Written explanation:
% The error in (c) is TRUNCATION error: it comes from deliberately stopping an infinite,
% mathematically exact series after a finite number of terms, so computing more terms always
% recovers more of the true value and shrinks the error. The error in (d) is ROUND-OFF error:
% it comes from how floating-point numbers are actually stored, since adding a small x = 0.2
% to an enormous 1e15 loses almost all of x's precision the moment the addition happens (long
% before log() or the subtraction ever runs), so the two nearly-equal log() results subtract
% down to almost nothing useful. Because this error is baked into the arithmetic itself rather
% than into which formula or how many series terms were chosen, no number of additional Taylor
% terms could ever fix it -- the fix has to be a different, numerically stable way of computing
% the same quantity, not a more accurate series.
